  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The School Office Register",
    "overview": "Calm, conventional public-service software: the category standard executed well, in the spirit of the GOV.UK Design System. It should read as trustworthy school administration that a head teacher, a tired subject teacher with 70 learners, and a project examiner all take seriously. White cards on a pale grey-blue ground, one navy accent, one legible sans-serif, and status told in words inside tinted pills. Nothing is decorative; every colour carries a meaning or a competency.\n\nThe system must stay conventional. This is a binding brand commitment (PRODUCT.md, user decision 10 Nov 2026): the earlier expressive \"Kampala signwriting\" direction was rejected as too dramatic and has been removed. Do not reintroduce expressive display type, painted panels, decorative motifs, or a second accent. New screens extend the incumbent pattern set (page header, panel, panel head, KPI card, tag, competency dot, trend chart) rather than inventing new surfaces.\n\nDensity is moderate and table-friendly: staff screens favour scannable lists and tables on laptops, and collapse to one column on low-cost Android phones. Pages are light: system-hosted font files, inline SVG charts, no imagery beyond the crest.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White panels with a hairline border and a whisper of shadow on a #f4f6f9 ground.",
      "A single navy accent for primary actions, links, the active nav item and focus.",
      "Atkinson Hyperlegible throughout, bold sentence-case headings, quiet size steps.",
      "Status always as a word in a tinted pill; colour never carries meaning alone.",
      "Competencies identified by a small colour dot plus their name.",
      "Inline SVG trend charts on the fixed CBC scale 0.9–3.0."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "Navy is the only accent. Primary actions, links, active nav and focus share it; no second brand colour is introduced. The theme tokens stay swappable so official school colours can replace navy when supplied.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Small Hue Rule",
        "body": "Competency colours appear only as small dots (10px circles), thin bars (8px tall, full-round) and chart lines/points. Never as fills for panels, buttons, text, or backgrounds.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Word First Rule",
        "body": "Every status colour sits inside a pill that also carries a word (High, Medium, Low, Needs action, Intervention under way, Escalated, Improved). Colour reinforces; it never informs alone.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
